   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law, in relation to permitting certain inmate releases to occur on a Thursday to ensure next-day reporting",
    "basePrintNo": "S4905",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4905-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §74, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4905\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to permitting certain\ninmate releases to occur on a Thursday to ensure next-day reporting\n\nPurpose of the Bill:\n\nTo grant to the Commissioner of Corrections and Community Supervision\nthe authority in certain cases to advance an inmate's scheduled release\ndate from a Friday to a Thursday, in order to ensure next day reporting\nto a community supervision location.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nCorrection Law Section 74 would be amended to accomplish the above-stat-\ned purpose.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nCorrection Law Section 74 in effect specifies that when an inmate's\nrelease to the community is scheduled to occur on a Saturday or Sunday,\nit shall be deemed to fall on the preceding Friday.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nWhen a person is released from incarceration and placed under super-\nvision in the community, it is a fundamental precept that in order to\nensure an effective transition from being an inmate to becoming a paro-\nlee, such person not has any undue delay between the date of release and\nthe report date to a community supervision location. Too often, when a\nrelease occurs on a Friday and the report date can't happen until the\nfollowing Monday, thus leaving an entire weekend before the first\ncontact is made with a community supervision official, the risk that the\nparolee may violate one or more conditions of parole supervision in an\nimportant respect becomes significantly elevated. For example, the temp-\ntation for the offender to possibly celebrate his or release with family\nor friends by drinking an alcoholic beverage or taking an illegal\nsubstance over the weekend, before a report may have occurred, is very\nreal. Public safety will be enhanced if the Commissioner is granted the\nauthority to advance the release date from a Friday to a Thursday, in\norder to ensure that a next day report by the parolee to a community\nsupervision location can take place. This discretion would not be needed\nin any scenario where a person is released on a Friday without any peri-\nod of supervision to be served in the community.\n\nWhen the person first reports to a reporting center, all of the terms\nand conditions of supervision are reviewed with the parolee a parole\nofficer. Among other things, the parolee is reminded that the underlying\nsentence of imprisonment is still in effect and running, and that it is\ncritical for the parolee to comply with all conditions of supervision,\nin order to avoid the possibility of re-incarceration. This includes\nrefraining from any consumption of alcohol or use of illegal substances.\nThe first twenty-four hours after release from a facility can be the\nmost critical for a parolee, and therefore, a next day reporting\nrequirement is one of the best ways to ensure a successful outcome.\n\nBudget implications:\n\nNone.\n\nLocal impact:\n\nNone.\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThirty days after enactment.\n\n",
